# API Route Structure

All API routes live in `src/app/api/` following Next.js App Router conventions.

## Permission Helpers (use these, don't roll your own auth)

```ts
// Most routes — checks role + trial status:
const result = await requirePermission("members", "create");
if (result instanceof NextResponse) return result;
const { organizationId, role } = result;

// Owner-only routes (billing, plans, org settings):
const result = await requireOwner();
if (result instanceof NextResponse) return result;

// Any authenticated user, no RBAC check:
const result = await requireAuth();
if (result instanceof NextResponse) return result;
```

All three come from `@/lib/api-permissions`. `requirePermission` also enforces trial expiry (returns 402).

## Response Conventions

- Success: `NextResponse.json(data)` — 200 default, or explicit `{ status: 201 }` for creates
- Errors: always `NextResponse.json({ error: "message" }, { status: NNN })`
- Status codes: 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden, 402 Trial expired, 404 Not found, 500 Server error
- Wrap handler body in try/catch; log with `console.error` and return 500 on unexpected errors

## Supabase in API Routes

Use `createClient()` from `@/lib/supabase/server` (session-aware).
Use `createAdminClient()` from `@/lib/supabase/admin` only for privileged ops that bypass RLS.

## Resources and Actions

Defined in `@/lib/permissions.ts`. Valid resources: `members | visits | organization | staff | invitations | event_types | event_categories | billing | plans | profile`.
Valid actions: `view | create | update | delete | archive | export | invite | remove | manage`.
